The 25 seconds is not a single slow call — it's the cumulative cost of repeated existsSync invocations during plugin discovery. Three files dominate:

src/plugins/bundle-manifest.ts — 18 calls (e.g. resolveCodexSkillDirs, hasCursorMcpCapability, manifest format probing)
src/plugins/bundled-dir.ts — 14 calls (workspace root probing, override resolution)
src/plugins/sdk-alias.ts — 9 calls (SDK alias candidate resolution)

In every case, the path being checked is read-only during the plugin scan phase, and many paths are checked multiple times — for example, bundle-manifest.ts checks path.join(rootDir, "skills") twice (lines 145 and 158 in the original source) within a single plugin's manifest resolution.
What this PR changes

Introduces src/shared/cached-fs.ts exporting existsSyncCached(p) and invalidateExistsSyncCache(p?).
Replaces 41 fs.existsSync(...) call sites with existsSyncCached(...) across the three files above.

The cache is process-level. Cached values are never invalidated automatically — the assumption is that paths checked in the plugin scan phase are stable. invalidateExistsSyncCache is exposed for tests and for future callers that mutate paths and want subsequent checks to hit the disk.
control-ui-assets.ts was intentionally not modified — it uses controlUiFsRuntime.existsSync (a different abstraction layer) rather than fs.existsSync directly. Worth a follow-up but out of scope for this PR.
Validation
CPU profile was captured by setting NODE_OPTIONS="--cpu-prof --cpu-prof-dir=..." and running openclaw agent --local --message "ping" --timeout 30 to a deliberate session-arg error (which is enough to exercise the full plugin discovery path).
Profiles were inspected in speedscope (Sandwich view, sorted by total time).
